# CLAUDE.md.example

> A `CLAUDE.md` is like a toothbrush: useful daily, but don't use someone else's. This is a starting point, not a config to copy wholesale. Take it to your project (or `~/.claude/`) root, throw out what doesn't fit *your* mouth, and fill in the `<placeholders>` with your own habits.
>
> **First line must be `# <Project Name>`.** Not a Claude Code rule, but skills in this repo (`cowork:save-work`, `cowork:switch`) read it to identify the project. (This file uses `# CLAUDE.md.example` as its own title; replace it.)
>
> Sections marked **[core]** are near-universal — keep them. Sections marked **[pattern]** showcase a technique; adopt if it fits. `<!-- example: ... -->` blocks show the *shape* of a personal rule so you can write your own — they are not defaults.
>
> **Design principle — keep this file lean.** It loads in full at every session start, reaches your sub-agents, and is re-injected after `/compact`. Target < 200 lines (the hard cap is 4 MiB, irrelevant in practice); measure with `/context`, not by character count. It holds only *triggers + standing biases*; bulky detail goes elsewhere — see the last section for where.
>
> Companion files live in [`examples/.claude/`](examples/.claude/): copy them to your own `.claude/` and drop the `.example` suffix.

## Security 🔒 [core]

Never read/parse files holding secrets (keys, tokens, credentials, `.env`, certs). Non-obvious ones too: `.npmrc`, `.pypirc`, cloud service-key JSON. If asked → warn + require explicit confirmation.

## Communication [core]

Clarify before big tasks; never hallucinate; say "I don't know" over speculation.

**Concision**: verdict + the facts that change a decision; detail on request. Cut preamble, process narration, discarded options, repeats. Never cut: risk, uncertainty, failure/blocker. In doubt → keep.

<!-- example: message-head shortcuts [pattern] — a keyword at the start of a message switches the reply mode. Name them whatever you like.

**`eli5 <topic>`** (alone = re-explain the last turn): plain words for a senior practitioner, 8-12 lines of prose, no diagrams/jargon, one useful analogy or none.

**`tldr`**: session status — done / in progress / blocked / decision — terse bullets, no table. Source = this session, not a disk scan. Nothing in progress → say so.
-->

## Checkpoint trailer [pattern]

When a turn settles a decision, reveals a non-obvious learning, rejects a path, shifts frame, or leaves an open fork, END with one greppable HTML comment starting `<!-- ckpt`. Skip on clarifying/ack/pure-tool turns. Clauses → `.claude/refs/checkpoint.md`.

<!-- Required by dstoic:checkpoint, dstoic:save-context and ring: they harvest these trailers for free instead of re-reading the transcript. Without this rule they fall back to a slower, lossier extractive pass. Keep the `<!-- ckpt` marker stable — the skills grep it. -->

## Execution defaults [core]

Classify the task first, then match the tool — don't start big and walk back, or start blind and escalate through failure. Genuinely ambiguous/big/novel → top tier to frame it. When a call is non-trivial, route it deliberately instead of guessing:

- **model / effort** — don't default to your most expensive model. Route by task shape (chores → cheapest · single-file plumbing → mid-tier · strategy / hard refactor / security audit → top tier · very large context → long-context model). Changing *effort* keeps the prompt cache (cheap); switching *model* breaks it (costly). If you have a model-selection skill, invoke it here.
- **sub-agents / fan-out** — delegate to isolate context. Sweeping many files where only the conclusion matters → fan out to a cheaper model. Independent per-item work → parallel sub-agents. Never shard cross-item reasoning or final synthesis — those stay on the main thread. If you have an execution-topology skill, invoke it here.
- **costly fan-outs** (multi-source web research, multi-agent workflows): ALWAYS confirm scope with the user before launching; never auto-invoke.

<!-- The bullets above name *capabilities*, not skill slugs, so this file stays portable. If your routing detail is bulky, move the table into a skill (its description auto-fires) or a .claude/refs/ file, and keep only the trigger here. -->

## Planning [core]

**Boulder → Pebbles**: boulders (multi-step, architectural, or ambiguous) get planned into trackable stories before work starts; pebbles (single file, obvious) just get done.

## Output style [core]

Three audiences, three styles:

- **Conversation** (CLI replies): see Concision above.
- **Human deliverables** (docs, reports, slides): visual-first — diagrams for flows/decisions/comparisons; structured data as clean YAML/JSON. Formatting dresses the prose, it doesn't replace it.
- **LLM-facing artifacts** (context files, specs, agent prompts): token-optimized. Strip prose, keep directives/facts/structure. Diagrams only for complex flows. No decoration.

<!-- example: no hard wrap [pattern]
**No hard wrap**: one paragraph = one line (the reader wraps). Except code/YAML/tables.
-->
<!-- example: deliverable prose rules [pattern] — your own voice, e.g.
Full sentences with conjugated verbs, one idea per sentence, readable aloud. Ban <your tics: slogan appositions, decorative triads, ...>.
-->
> example: diagram syntax guard [pattern] — only if you render Mermaid (kept out of an HTML comment because the arrow syntax would close it)
>
> **Mermaid:** quote labels `["Label"]` · multi-target `A -->|l| B & C` · define nodes inside a subgraph before referencing them · colored fills need `color:#000` (`classDef ok fill:#90EE90,stroke:#333,color:#000`).

